This document is an attempt at a curriculum covering the basic use of the empty hand#footnote[Throughout this document, I use the term 'empty hand', instead of 'off hand' or similar. I do so to emphasise it as an essential component of fencing with the rapier 'alone', rather than an unfortunate byproduct of human physiology.] in Italian rapier fencing.
It consists of four sections: principles, knife work, rapier work, and primary source plays.
The first section sets out the principles which the remainder of the document attempts to put into practice.
The second sections contains a collection of drills and free play exercises to be done with training knives.
This is not an attempt at teaching knife fighting, but a way of teaching and practising mechanics and movement patterns without the added complexity of maneouvering a 110cm length of steel.
The third section contains drills and free play exercises focused on using the empty hand with the rapier.
The fourth section contains a collection of plays from various primary sources that use the empty hand.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[How To Use this Document]
Each section of this document builds on the previous: the principles laid out in section one lay the foundation for the mechanics and movements in section two on knife work.
The second section contains drills and exercises to familiarise the student with using the empty hand.
Once those mechanics have become familiar, the third section combines them with the blade- and footwork of Italian rapier fencing.
Finally, the plays at the end of the third section integrate the empty hand as part of longer sequences.
Each drill is focused on a single technique and several drills can be combined to form decision drills.
The free play exercises are meant to be a transition between drilling and sparring; they are constrained and often asymmetrical, but should be carried out with increasing intensity.
The point, is not, however, to 'win' these exercises, it is to begin applying techniques from the drills under pressure.

In an ideal world, the sections should be covered in order over the course of several weeks.
That, however, is hardly viable when teaching a rapier class in a fencing club.
The next best thing is to pick out a combination of knife and rapier drills and exercises covering the same topic, and use that as the basis for a class.
Even so, I would advice against introducing the primary source plays before the class has a good grip of the fundamentals.

#heading(
  level: 4,
  numbering: none,
)[A Note for (and against) Left-handed Fencers]
As always, drills and exercises need to be adapted when fencing (against) an opponent who wields their weapon in a different hand than you do.
For some drills in this document, it should suffice to switch the side of attack (inside/outside), for others, I have made a note suggesting a modified version.
Even so, there might be exercises that are simply not appropriate when fencing 'leftie' against 'rightie'.
In those cases, trying to force a technique to work is a waste of time, and the better solution is for the fencer on the receiving end to fence with their 'other strong hand'.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Terminology]
Broadly speaking, there are four ways to use the empty hand in Italian rapier fencing: parrying, taking over, trapping, and displacing.#footnote[Fencing terminology is infamously inconsistent and this topic is no exception. These are the terms I have settled on.]
In an empty-hand parry, the opponent's weapon is deflected first, and usually only, with the empty hand.
This is often combined with a simultaneous (#smallcaps[stesso tempo]) attack with the rapier.
As a rule of thumb, empty-hand parries do not work against cuts.#footnote[They can, but it is a bit sketchy. See e.g. #cite(<digrasssiHisTrueArte2000>, form: "author")'s #index[di Grassi, Giacomo] _His True Arte of Defence_.]
When the opponent's blade is parried or displaced with the rapier, the empty hand can take over controlling their weapon, freeing up the rapier for offensive use.
Trapping is any form of taking control over the opponent's weapon.
This ranges from the complex traps and disarms found in some Spanish sources to simply grabbing any part of the opponent's weapon within reach.#footnote[Opinions differ on whether it is possible to hold on to a sharpened rapier or not; to my knowledge, it has been some time since anyone tried it out.]
Finally, the empty hand can be used to displace the opponent's blade and create an opening (similarly to a #smallcaps[battuta]).

While there are examples of all four types of empty-hand actions in Italian rapier treatises from the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, parrying and taking over are by far the most common.
For that reason, they are the focus of this document.
Although it seems like an obvious thing to do, I am not aware of treatise describing the offensive use of the empty hand in the form of punching, slapping, eye-poking, etc.
Whether that accurately reflects how people fenced at the time is another matter altogether.

=== Principles

It seems to me that there are three fundamental principles for successfully integrating the empty hand with the rapier:
+ The empty hand must not get in the way of the rapier.
+ The empty hand must be ready to use without any preparatory movement.
+ The empty hand must be an intentional part of every action.
These three principles are in addition to the general principles of fencing in general and rapier fencing in particular.

From (1.) it follows that the empty hand is most easily used on the inside.
On the inside, any thrusting attack to the head and torso can be deflected with the same movement of the empty hand: a semicircle from just above the head to somewhere outside the left hip.
Using the same motion to cover the entire inside also means that the movement removes a decision point, as opposed to holding the empty hand in the middle and moving it up or down depending on the opponent's target.
Doing this motion from the top down rather than the bottom up means that gravity works with you and against your opponent.
Depending on context, it can be useful to 'tilt' this imaginary semicircle towards the opponent, by moving the end point forward and upward.
In this way, the parrying movement can also be used to displace and to take over when the opponent's blade is on the inside.

From this in combination with (2.), it follows that, in the guard, the empty hand should be held somewhere around head-height and close to the face.
Assuming a _capoferrian_ guard, the body must be angled so that the empty hand can protect both face and torso without having to turn the torso first.
Exactly what this looks like will vary from fencer to fencer.

Parrying, displacing, and taking over on the outside come with some downsides, but should nonetheless be part of one's repertoire.
The most obvious downside is that they require crossing the arms, thus necessarily limiting the weapon arm's movement upward or downward.
Another downside is that they require more precision.
Using the sweeping motion described above, actions on the inside have a wide margin of error---a parry will work whether one catches the opponent's blade with the hand, wrist, or forearm.
Such a motion is not practical on the outside, instead actions on the outside tend to be horizontal.
At the same time, most rapier schools use a guard where the outside is 'closed'.
Attacks on the outside therefore tend to follow from invitations, #smallcaps[battuti] or feints.
By drilling responses to these that incorporate the empty hand, one can alleviate some of those downsides.

#v(1.5em, weak: true)
So much for platonic fencing.
These are principles, not commandments and they will not apply to every situation.#footnote[Such as your opponent's avoiding your empty-hand parry by lifting their tip over your hand, as is suggested in _Den Ridderlige og Adelige Fecht-Konstis_ #index[_Den Ridderlige og Adelige Fecht-Konstis_] (#cite(<a.RidderligeOcAdelige1646>, form: "year")). Any empty-hand action immediately following that will likely start from below, as that is where the hand is.]
Nonetheless, this document is concerned with the basics, and for that these principles will apply nine times out of ten.

=== Knife Work

#index-start[Knife work]
As I wrote in the introduction, this section does not describe a system of knife fighting.
Many of the drills and patterns are taken from or inspired by various knife systems, but the goal here is to get used to using both hands in concert, not to become proficient at using a knife.
Working with knives when learning to use the empty hand has several advantages.
For one, knives are much shorter and much lighter than rapiers.
This makes the discrepancy between the hands is smaller.
The lighter weight also allows more repetitions of new movements before fatigue becomes an issue.
The most important distinction between knife training and rapier fencing, however, is that knife fighting is not fencing---there is no fancy footwork, no seventeenth-century flair.

Although not all knives are created equal and the same goes for training knives, each different implement affords different ways of training.
I make the people in my class work with all of them---plastic bowies, LARP daggers, aluminium trainers, rolled-up newspapers, etc.
A trainer I find especially useful for higher-intensity work is a \~16cm roll of yoga mat, wrapped in gaffer's tape so that only 1--2cm are exposed at one end.#footnote[I have taken this idea from Scott Babb's knife fighting system, _Libre_.]
It is cheap, stiff enough to be usable, yet soft enough that neither student nor teacher need to worry about anyone getting hurt.

As for posture, grip, and footwork: keep it simple.
Feet shoulder-width apart, weight on the balls of your feet, slight bend in the knees, shoulders almost square to the opponent/partner, posture relatively upright, weapon slightly in front of the body but not too far, and empty hand has described above.#footnote[This is intentionally vague. Find something that allows you to move well and is distinctly different from your rapier guard. Don't overthink it.]
Hold the weapon point down or point up (hammer or sabre grip to suit preference and implement) depending on exercise and do not lock the wrist.
In terms of footwork, think boxing or Filipino triangle footwork, not fencing.
And whatever you do, do not lunge!

==== Drills
===== Dui Tempi
+ Implement: larger blade, e.g. bowie held point up.

  A attacks with a diagonal cut to the head (number '1' or '2').

  B blocks with a cut to the forearm, takes over the parry with the empty hand, and strikes A with a cut or a thrust.

  *Note*: experiment with different placements of the empty hand in relation to the blade.
+ As above, but A attacks with a thrust to the face or chest.
+ Implement: smaller blade, preferably held point down.

  A attacks with a diagonal thrust to the outside of B's neck.

  B blocks with the forearm of their weapon hand.

  A clears the block out of the way (downwards) and finishes the attack.

  *Note*: this drill and the following does not work R against L.

+ As above, but B blocks with their empty hand.

  A clears the parry again, this time more sidewards than downwards.

  *Flow*: this drill can be combined with number 3 into a flow/reaction drill.
  Depending on B's block, A will have to clear differently.
  B can also block first with the weapon hand and then with their empty hand, forcing A to clear twice.
+ As 3 and 4 above, but A's attack is aimed at the inside of B's neck.

  *Flow*: These two can be combined with 3 and 4 to create a flow drill where both A and B have to react to each other.
+ Implement: smaller blade, held point down.

  A attacks with a forehand to B's face/neck.

  B blocks with the forearm of their empty hand, seeking to counter (_in due tempi_) to A's trunk.

  A rips down through B's block, while either checking their weapons shoulder or delivering a palm strike to their face.

  *Note*: This drill is not suitable leftie against rightie.

+ Implement: small or large blade, point up or point down, especially useful with a bowie.

  A attacks with a forehand to B's face/neck.

  B blocks with their weapon hand (or a stop-cut to the arm with a bowie).

  A clears B's block to the outside with their empty hand before it connects.

  *Note:* This clearing works best from below.

===== Stesso Tempo
+ Implement: blade of any size, held point up or point down.

  A attacks with a diagonal cut to the head (number '1' or '2').

  B steps toward A while simultaneously blocking the attack with the empty hand and striking A with a cut or thrust.

  *Note*: try out different forms of empty-hand blocks---hand, forearm, trapping, or the kind of elbow 'crash' found in systems like _UC_ and _SPEAR_.
+ Implement: blade of any size, held point up or point down.

  A attacks with a thrust to the trunk.

  B parries with their empty hand while striking A with a cut or a thrust.

+ Implement: blade of any size, held point up or point down.

  A attacks with a horizontal cut/thrust to the outside face or neck.

  B steps in, thrusting A in the chest while moving the empty hand across to the outside of their face, checking A's arm/elbow.

===== Free Play
Free play exercises fall somewhere between sparring and drilling.
On one hand, they are meant to stress test techniques and concepts that have been drilled.
On the other, they are an opportunity to experiment with ways of getting to those techniques.
To that end, the intensity of the exercise should be adjusted to match the goal---less for experimentation, more for stress testing.
In any case, the focus should remain on using both hands together, not on winning.

+ A fights as usual, but B cannot use their empty hand.
+ As above, but B is up against a wall.#footnote[The focus here should be on using weapon and empty hand in concert to safely close the distance. To that end, use control, not speed and explosiveness.]
+ As above, but it is A against the wall.
+ Both can use the empty hand, but one is against the wall.
+ Fight as usual, but in a confined space.
+ Limited targeting: the only valid targets are e.g. the back, the face, or the trunk.
+ Multiple hits: Do not reset after each hit, but continue until someone has been hit multiple times.
+ As above, but pursue a situation where the opponent is unable to counter, even if given tempo to do so.
#index-end[Knife work]

=== Rapier Work
Before attempting any of the drills below, spend some time integrating the lessons learned from the knife work into your rapier basics---especially stance and footwork.
Look for the feeling of symmetry between weapon hand and empty hand that has hopefully developed with the knife.

The following drills differ in some ways from the plays traditionally found in rapier treatises.
They are short.
Few have more than three steps.
This is to keep the focus on the technique of movement being drilled.
I have also tried to emphasise offensive actions, as the treatises tend to emphasise defensive ones.
Much like primary source plays, however, not every drill is likely to work in sparring, and not every drill will work for every fencer.

==== Drills
===== Dui Tempi
+ A attacks with a diagonal cut to the head or neck (number '1' or '2').

  B parries in #smallcaps[quarta] or #smallcaps[secunda],#footnote[Following the convention of _Den Ridderlige og Adelige Fecht-Konstis_ #index[_Den Ridderlige og Adelige Fecht-Konstis_] #cite(<a.RidderligeOcAdelige1646>, form: "year"), parries are denoted by the orientation of the blade. Thus, a parry in #smallcaps[secunda] is what later systems call a #smallcaps[terz]-parry.] depending on the attack, takes over the parry with the empty hand, and strikes A with a cut or a thrust.

  *Note*: experiment with different placements of the empty hand in relation to the blade.
+ As above, but A attacks with a thrust to the face or chest.
+ As above, but A attacks with an inside thrust to the abdomen, and B parries in hanging #smallcaps[prima] or low #smallcaps[quarta].
+ A attacks with a thrust to the abdomen in #smallcaps[terza] or #smallcaps[secunda].

  B parries with a hanging #smallcaps[secunda] to their outside, steps forward with a #smallcaps[passata]/Profilwechsel, grips the hilt or base of A's blade, and simultaneously pulls with their empty hand while thrusting with the rapier under A's blade.
  Ideally, A's blade should be trapped between B's empty hand and their quillons.

  *Note*: depending on the length of the rapier, it may be necessary to keep the weapon hand relatively far back during the #smallcaps[passata].

  *Variation 1*: instead of thrusting with the point, B can keep their rapier point-down and hit A in the face with the hilt.
  This is particularly nasty if the rapier has straight quillons.
+ As above, but instead of levering A's blade between empty hand and own quillons, B frees their blade, lifts their arm behind them, and thrust A from above.
+ As above, but instead of gripping A's blade, B pushes the elbow of A's weapon arm.#footnote[Cf. the _Fiore de'i Liberi_ #index[_Fiore de\'i Liberi_] adaptation below.] This is especially effective if the rapier parry has the force of a #smallcaps[battuta].
+ A attacks on the inside with a diagonal cut to the head or shoulder.

  B parries with a hanging parry (#smallcaps[octav]), steps forward with the back leg and grips A's forearm with their empty hand. Riposte at will.

+ Same as above, but A attacks on the outside and B parries accordingly (#smallcaps[septim]).

===== Stesso Tempo
+ A engages B with #smallcaps[stringere] on the inside, seeking to close the distance.

  As soon as B's point comes within reach, A simultaneously clears it out of the way and thrusts B in #smallcaps[quarta].

  *Note*: 'had B been a shrewd person' they would have never let A get that close. Well, B isn't always shrewd.

+ A engages B with #smallcaps[stringere] on the outside, seeking to provoke a disengage.

  B disengages to the inside when they feel forced to do so.

  As soon as B disengages, A advances or lunges, while simultaneously clearing B's rapier with the empty hand and thrusting B in #smallcaps[quarta] or #smallcaps[secunda] (see note).

  *Note*: Depending on how early B disengages, A's timing will change drastically.
  Making the final thrust in #smallcaps[quarta] will provide A with more protection, while thrusting in #smallcaps[secunda] will leave slightly more space for the empty hand to work.

+ As above, but B thrusts in opposition directly following their disengage.

  As A parries and thrusts, they can either stand their ground or step to their inside with a traverso.#footnote[Yes, this is towards, not away from, the incoming attack. The idea is to further displace the opponents blade, while keeping it under control.]

+ A thrusts at B.

  B simultaneously parries and counters with a thrust.

===== Free Play
+ A fences as usual, but B cannot use their empty hand.
+ As above, but B is up against a wall.
+ As above, but it is A against the wall.
+ Both fencers can use their empty hand, but one has their back against the wall.
+ Multiple hits: Do not reset after each hit, but continue for until someone has been hit multiple times.
+ As above, but pursue a situation where the opponent is unable to counter, even if given tempo to do so.

=== Primary Source Plays

#index-start[Primary source plays]
The following plays have been adapted from a variety of rapier treatises.
Where the author gives several responses to an action, only those using the empty hand have been included.
For the sake of brevity, I have rewritten them in a more schematic form, but I highly recommend consulting the original sources, or translations thereof.

#cite(<capoferroRidolfoCapoFerro>, form: "author") #index[Capo Ferro, Ridolfo] plays are based on Wilson and Swanger's translation.
The #cite(<FioreLiberiSword>, form: "author") _Fiore de'i Liberi_ #index[_Fiore de\'i Liberi_] play is based on translations by Michael Chidester and Colin Hatcher.
The #cite(<Saviolo_1595>, form: "author") #index[Saviolo, Vincentio] sequence is based on the English original.
All three are available on #link("https://wiktenauer.com")[Wiktenauer]#index[Wiktenauer].

==== Capo Ferro No. 20

#index-start[Capo Ferro, Ridolfo]
#index-start("Primary source plays", "Capo Ferro: plate No. 20")

#sourced-figure(
  image("../assets/Capo_Ferro_20.png", width: 95%),
  caption: [Plate 20 from Capo Ferro's _Gran Simulacro_.],
  source: "https://wiktenauer.com/wiki/File:Capo_Ferro_20.png",
  tag: "capoferro20",
)

C #smallcaps[stringere] D on the outside.

D disengages, seeking to strike C with a #smallcaps[stoccata].

*Option 1*: C parries in #smallcaps[quarta] with a beat of the front foot.#footnote[I take this to mean an _appell_, but also a transfer of weight to the front foot.]
C then strikes D's face in #smallcaps[seconda] with a #smallcaps[passata], while gripping D's wrist with the empty hand from above.

*Option 2*: C parries in #smallcaps[quarta] while closing with the back foot.
C then strikes D's face in #smallcaps[seconda] with a lunge, while gripping D's wrist with the empty hand from above.
#index-end("Primary source plays", "Capo Ferro: plate No. 20")
#index-end[Capo Ferro, Ridolfo]

==== _Fiore de'i Liberi_ 14d

#index-start("Primary source plays", "Fiore de'i Liberi: 14d")
#index-start[_Fiore de\'i Liberi_]_Fiore de'i Liberi_ is a treatise from before rapiers were a thing and, as such, the fencing it describes differs somewhat in style from Italian rapier treatises.
This technique still works, however, especially for more athletic fencers.
I have included it here as an example of a more forceful use of the empty hand.

#sourced-figure(
  image("../assets/Pisani-Dossi_MS_14a-d.png", width: 95%),
  caption: [Illustration 14d from the _Pisani Dossi_ manuscript.],
  source: "https://wiktenauer.com/images/7/7f/Pisani-Dossi_MS_14a-d.png",
  tag: "fiore14d",
)

A attacks B on the inside with a thrust in #smallcaps[prima] or #smallcaps[secunda].

B parries in #smallcaps[quarta].

As B parries, A steps forward with a #smallcaps[passata] and shoves B's elbow across their body.

B then 'wrap[s his] sword around [A's] neck from behind, and [A] will have no defense to this.'

*Note 1*: A's initial thrust can either be direct or somewhat diagonal, thus forcing B to parry further out than otherwise.
This can be combined with different angles in the #smallcaps[passata], to the point where it almost becomes a #smallcaps[traverso].

*Note 2*: I am aware of two illustrated versions of _Fiore de'i Liberi_.
In one, A is depicted with the right foot in front, in the other with the left foot in front.
Especially with long rapiers, I have found that a #smallcaps[passata] works better than a lunge.#index-end[_Fiore de\'i Liberi_]
#index-end("Primary source plays", "Fiore de'i Liberi: 14d")

==== Saviolo's Second Ward<empty-hand-saviolo-2>

#index-start("Primary source plays", "Saviolo: Second Ward")
#index-start[Saviolo, Vincentio]
This sequence from Saviolo's _His Practice_ (#cite(<Saviolo_1595>, form: "year")) makes for an excellent drill, with two caveats.
First, the sequence relies on Saviolo's somewhat idiosyncratic footwork.
#index-start("Saviolo, Vincentio", "Second ward")Second, it starts from his 'second ward'#index("Guard", "Saviolo's second ward") which he describes as follows, somewhat contrary to the accompanying illustration (@saviolo):

#set quote(block: true)
#quote(attribution: [#cite(
  <Saviolo_1595>,
  supplement: "17",
  form: "prose",
)])[[The scholler must] place his right legge formoste, a little bending the knee, so that the heele of his right foote stand just against the midst of his left foote, holding his swoord hand close on the outside of his right knee, with his swoorde helde in shorte, least his adversarye should gaine the same, ever keeping the poynte directlye on the face or bellye of his enemye]

#sourced-figure(
  image("../assets/Saviolo_2.jpg", width: 95%),
  caption: [Saviolo's second ward.],
  source: "https://wiktenauer.com/images/archive/3/3b/20130915232754%21Saviolo_2.jpg",
  tag: "saviolo",
)
#index-end("Saviolo, Vincentio", "Second ward")

M #smallcaps[stoccata] with a small step right around the circle.

S #smallcaps[stoccata] and empty-hand parry with a step right around the circle in counter-time.

M right foot counter-time, #smallcaps[imbroccata] to S's face and empty-hand parry with a left-foot step toward S's left hand.

S empty-hand parry ('beating the thrust ... outward toward the left side') with step right around the circle in counter-time, then #smallcaps[imbroccata] to M's face.

M empty-hand parry while stepping his right foot towards S's left hand in counter-time and then #smallcaps[stoccata] to S's belly.

S empty-hand parry downward while stepping right foot towards M's left hand in counter-time and then #smallcaps[stoccata] to M's belly

M empty-hand parry to their right side, stepping diagonally backwards with their right foot in counter-time and #smallcaps[stoccata] to S's head.
#index-end[Saviolo, Vincentio]
#index-end("Primary source plays", "Saviolo: Second Ward")
#index-end[Primary source plays]
